Another example, suppose it's just like Andrew's example, but someone designing the microcontroller code for the scale had a bug, so 50 % of the time the display rounds to the nearest 1 lb, 40% of the time it rounds UP, and 10% of the time it rounds DOWN.

Now, you could put a maxent prior on the roundoff errors that's a mixture model of the 3 possibilities, and that would be a step-function, but of course HMC isn't going to like that. So you could graph this step function and design a sort of smoothish approximation to that step function, and then write

for(i in 1:N)
  increment_log_prob(my_smooth_approximation(roundoff_parameter[i]));

Or you could write a weird likelihood, and try to back-out the rounding errors with weird generated quantities...

Generally, it makes sense to me to write your model so you have parameters that describe the unknown quantities of interest, and priors on those parameters that describe the information you have about them going into the analysis.

On Sunday, August 23, 2015 at 2:34:30 PM UTC-4, andre.p...@googlemail.com wrote:
> The pairs plot looks not so good, but I have no idea how
> to fix the correlations between the parameters.

High correlations between parameters, by themselves, are not a problem. The main problem you are looking for in a pairs plot is where divergent transitions or transitions that exceed max_treedepth occur, but there do not seem to be any in this case.

The multimodality (probably across chains) is a problem and is evident in the pairs plot. The solution is likely to do a variant of the Matt trick for AR1 processes. Instead of declaring a vector parameter nu and doing

segment(nu,2,T-1) ~ normal(mu+segment(y_r,1,T-1)*phi, sigma);

in the model block. You should declare a vector parameter nu_z, a local vector nu, and do

nu[1] <- mu / (1 - phi) + sigma / sqrt(1 - square(phi)) * nu_z[1];
for (t in 2:T) nu[t] <- mu + phi * y[t-1] + sigma * nu_z[t];
nu_z ~ normal(0,1);
y ~ poisson_log(nu);

That way you have no prior dependence among the elements of nu_z and no prior dependence with mu and phi.

